/**
 * Famous islands and peninsulas for the world-map quizzes (see src/quizzes/landformQuizzes.js).
 * Pure data, also read by scripts/build-landforms.mjs, which pre-renders the maps into
 * public/data/landforms/<quiz>.json.
 *
 * name = English, ro = Romanian (when different).
 * alt = other spellings accepted in English (plus native names), roAlt = the same in Romanian;
 * without roAlt, Romanian accepts `alt` too. "Peninsula X" / "X Peninsula" / "Insula X" also
 * work as plain "X" (see landformQuizzes.js).
 * Geometry (only used by the build script):
 *   at:     [lon, lat] — the island is the Natural Earth 1:10m land polygon under this point
 *   ne:     name of a Natural Earth "geography regions" polygon (peninsulas)
 *   clip:   polygon [[lon, lat]…] cut out of the land; the biggest piece is kept
 *   marine: name(s) of Natural Earth marine polygons (seas, gulfs, straits) — all parts kept
 *   lake:   name(s) of Natural Earth 1:10m lakes — all parts kept
 *   plate:  name of a tectonic plate in Peter Bird's PB2002 model (all parts kept)
 *   color:  fill for the plate before it's found (the plates cover the whole map, so neighbours
 *           need different colours to stay apart)
 *   hist:   year of a historical map (scripts: hb/world_<year>.geojson) + names: the states that make up the item;
 *           the badge goes on the biggest piece
 *   r:      radius of a point marker, in degrees (default 0.45 — smaller for city maps)
 *   river:  name(s) of a river in Natural Earth's rivers (bbox: [w, s, e, n] when names repeat)
 *   line:   [[lon, lat], …] (or a list of those) — drawn as a line, not a shape; arrow: true adds
 *           an arrowhead at the end (ocean currents)
 *   point:  [lon, lat] — a small round marker (straits too narrow to draw: Hormuz, Bering…)
 *   label:  [lon, lat] — where its number goes, when the middle of the shape won't do (the Bering Sea
 *           is cut in two by the map's edge)
 */
